1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,420
a rest in remake industry, and then you go to a slave labor

2
00:00:02,420 --> 00:00:02,920
camp.

3
00:00:02,920 --> 00:00:03,840
So that's really bad.

4
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:06,000
None of this has got to be key to the legal system.

5
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,640
Just to point out, an interesting wage model

6
00:00:08,640 --> 00:00:10,600
is usually seen as a left-wing model,

7
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:13,760
but it immediately gives you an argument

8
00:00:13,760 --> 00:00:15,960
against unemployment insurance that

9
00:00:15,960 --> 00:00:18,880
exists on top of all the other ones about disincentives.

10
00:00:18,880 --> 00:00:22,040
So of course, the standard one, that unemployment insurance

11
00:00:22,040 --> 00:00:23,880
reduces the incentive to get a job.

12
00:00:23,880 --> 00:00:26,200
But then there's this one saying it also just increases

13
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:30,920
the unemployment rate by making unemployment so pleasant

14
00:00:30,920 --> 00:00:34,160
that you have to get more unemployment to make it scary

15
00:00:34,160 --> 00:00:35,280
again.

16
00:00:35,280 --> 00:00:37,520
And again, I'll suggest there's this kind of futility

17
00:00:37,520 --> 00:00:41,760
in unemployment insurance because you go and give it,

18
00:00:41,760 --> 00:00:44,160
and then that just causes the unemployment rate

19
00:00:44,160 --> 00:00:46,360
to go up in order to compensate to keep

20
00:00:46,360 --> 00:00:47,720
the level of fear the same.

21
00:00:51,280 --> 00:00:53,760
So there we go.

22
00:00:53,760 --> 00:00:56,840
So questions about efficiency wages?

23
00:00:56,840 --> 00:00:58,320
Copy efficiency wages?

24
00:01:03,280 --> 00:01:03,780
Oh, yeah.

25
00:01:03,780 --> 00:01:07,200
In general, if you go and read textbook accounts

26
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:09,600
of efficiency wages, at least half of them

27
00:01:09,600 --> 00:01:13,280
will just mess up and describe compensating differentials.

28
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:15,240
So for example, not that you're likely to,

29
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:17,120
but if you go and get Dornbush and Fischer's

30
00:01:17,120 --> 00:01:20,640
Intermediate Macroeconomics textbook from the early 90s,

31
00:01:20,640 --> 00:01:22,400
I've got it on my shelf, they have

32
00:01:22,400 --> 00:01:26,120
a discussion of efficiency wages that exactly describes

33
00:01:26,120 --> 00:01:27,800
compensating differentials.

34
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:31,800
And when I went to Bill Dickens and said,

35
00:01:31,800 --> 00:01:33,280
I said, look, there's no difference.

36
00:01:33,280 --> 00:01:34,640
He said, you don't know what you're talking about, right?

37
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:35,440
Of course there is.

38
00:01:35,440 --> 00:01:36,720
And I showed him the textbook.

39
00:01:36,720 --> 00:01:39,360
Look, these two fancy and famous guys from MIT,

40
00:01:39,360 --> 00:01:41,000
they're saying exactly what I'm saying.

41
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:43,680
And Bill said, well, they're just wrong.

42
00:01:43,680 --> 00:01:45,920
Oh, and then he went and showed me the math.

43
00:01:45,920 --> 00:01:47,320
I said, see, there is a difference.

44
00:01:47,320 --> 00:01:50,480
All right, well, why didn't they say that?

45
00:01:50,480 --> 00:01:53,680
Great question, so possibly a case

46
00:01:53,680 --> 00:01:56,840
where people were very, you know,

47
00:01:56,840 --> 00:01:59,920
really like a standard argument for using math in economics

48
00:01:59,920 --> 00:02:01,880
is you can express yourself clearly.

49
00:02:01,880 --> 00:02:04,320
But there's the problem, like all the back translation

50
00:02:04,320 --> 00:02:05,760
problem of when you do all the math

51
00:02:05,760 --> 00:02:08,120
and then try to explain the result in English again,

52
00:02:08,120 --> 00:02:10,040
a lot of people can no longer do it.

53
00:02:10,040 --> 00:02:11,960
So it might have actually been clearer

54
00:02:11,960 --> 00:02:14,680
not to have translated into math in the first place.

55
00:02:14,680 --> 00:02:16,920
You notice I didn't actually do the mathematical model

56
00:02:16,920 --> 00:02:19,200
efficiency wages.

57
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:22,000
So I say my version better than their version without the math

58
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:25,320
because what I said at least makes sense

59
00:02:25,320 --> 00:02:27,520
and was different from composition differentials.

60
00:02:27,520 --> 00:02:28,640
So which is the whole point of the model

61
00:02:28,640 --> 00:02:31,920
is to say something different from the really easy model

62
00:02:31,920 --> 00:02:36,040
that everyone should know from HRE.

63
00:02:36,040 --> 00:02:38,320
All right, we're now ready to move on

64
00:02:38,320 --> 00:02:41,160
to finance and portfolio theory.

65
00:02:41,160 --> 00:02:44,360
All right, so let's start with permanent income anomalies.

66
00:02:44,360 --> 00:02:45,800
First of all, who here is diminishing

67
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:48,280
marginal utility consumption?

68
00:02:48,280 --> 00:02:50,560
All right, most people have diminishing marginal utility

69
00:02:50,560 --> 00:02:51,760
consumption.

70
00:02:51,760 --> 00:02:54,400
Who here has access to intertemporal markets,

71
00:02:54,400 --> 00:02:55,920
such as a credit card?

72
00:02:55,920 --> 00:02:58,920
All right, or could get a credit card if you wanted one.

73
00:02:58,920 --> 00:03:00,720
If these two things are true, then it

74
00:03:00,720 --> 00:03:03,200
makes very little sense to tailor your current consumption

75
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:05,880
to your current income.

76
00:03:05,880 --> 00:03:10,400
Why should you base what you are consuming now

77
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:12,760
on how much money you are taking in right now when

78
00:03:12,760 --> 00:03:14,840
you could just go and borrow?

79
00:03:14,840 --> 00:03:16,400
So assume in grad school your income

80
00:03:16,400 --> 00:03:18,680
is low compared to what you expect for the rest of your

81
00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:20,960
life, so why not just go into debt,

82
00:03:20,960 --> 00:03:24,160
and then you can live nicely right now,

83
00:03:24,160 --> 00:03:25,660
and you'll have to pay it back later.

84
00:03:25,660 --> 00:03:28,160
But if you've got diminishing marginal utility consumption,

85
00:03:28,160 --> 00:03:30,960
isn't it better to smooth it out so that you

86
00:03:30,960 --> 00:03:33,360
have an intermediate lifestyle for your whole career

87
00:03:33,360 --> 00:03:35,880
rather than living in poverty now

88
00:03:35,880 --> 00:03:38,040
and then living in luxury later?

89
00:03:40,640 --> 00:03:43,080
So the smart thing to do is to base your consumption

90
00:03:43,080 --> 00:03:47,720
on your permanent or expected long-run income.

91
00:03:47,720 --> 00:03:52,240
So your permanent income, expected long-run income.

92
00:03:52,240 --> 00:03:54,740
And actually, this is the main thing that won Milton Friedman

93
00:03:54,740 --> 00:03:55,400
his Nobel Prize.

94
00:03:55,400 --> 00:03:58,240
Not monetarism, not creaches, not that stuff,

95
00:03:58,240 --> 00:04:01,080
but his work in the 50s on the permanent income hypothesis.

96
00:04:01,080 --> 00:04:03,120
That was what really won him the Nobel Prize.

97
00:04:03,120 --> 00:04:05,920
It's the main thing that got cited.

98
00:04:05,920 --> 00:04:08,440
And obviously, there's a lot of truth

99
00:04:08,440 --> 00:04:10,920
in this permanent income hypothesis.

100
00:04:10,920 --> 00:04:13,680
We don't know if this is that people base their consumption

101
00:04:13,680 --> 00:04:15,440
on their expected long-run income

102
00:04:15,440 --> 00:04:20,360
rather than what they happen to have earned this week.

103
00:04:20,360 --> 00:04:23,040
So for example, young people tend to build up a lot of debt,

104
00:04:23,040 --> 00:04:25,560
and they also tend to pay it off as they age.

105
00:04:25,560 --> 00:04:27,280
Well, none of that.

106
00:04:27,280 --> 00:04:29,120
And then when people retire, they generally

107
00:04:29,120 --> 00:04:33,960
consume out of assets they built up when they were working.

108
00:04:33,960 --> 00:04:36,120
All pretty well in fact.

109
00:04:36,120 --> 00:04:39,320
Experienced this before.

110
00:04:39,320 --> 00:04:41,720
But when behavioral economists have

111
00:04:41,720 --> 00:04:45,920
taken a closer look at the world of consumption and savings,

112
00:04:45,920 --> 00:04:47,600
they come up with a bunch of violations

113
00:04:47,600 --> 00:04:49,440
of the permanent income hypothesis.

114
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:51,640
So at least it doesn't seem to work perfectly.

115
00:04:51,640 --> 00:04:54,520
In fact, fairly far from perfectly.

116
00:04:54,520 --> 00:04:56,880
So for one thing, consumption does actually

117
00:04:56,880 --> 00:05:00,160
seem moderately sensitive to current income.

118
00:05:00,160 --> 00:05:01,960
So we look at medical students.

119
00:05:01,960 --> 00:05:04,200
Medical students have an enormously high graduation

120
00:05:04,200 --> 00:05:04,920
rate.

121
00:05:04,920 --> 00:05:07,920
It's like 95% of people that start medical school

122
00:05:07,920 --> 00:05:10,480
finish medical school.

123
00:05:10,480 --> 00:05:12,320
Really high.

124
00:05:12,320 --> 00:05:13,680
And doctors, you may have heard.

125
00:05:13,680 --> 00:05:15,760
And of course, if you would finish medical school,

126
00:05:15,760 --> 00:05:17,560
then you're very likely to become a doctor.

127
00:05:17,560 --> 00:05:21,400
And doctors are very likely to have very high income.

128
00:05:21,400 --> 00:05:23,480
So given that there's not much uncertainty that

129
00:05:23,480 --> 00:05:26,560
gets resolved during this process,

130
00:05:26,560 --> 00:05:28,680
you would think that medical students would just

131
00:05:28,680 --> 00:05:31,120
rack up piles of debt.

132
00:05:31,120 --> 00:05:32,600
And not just for medicals.

133
00:05:32,600 --> 00:05:35,960
Not the obvious one, where I can't get for medical school.

134
00:05:35,960 --> 00:05:37,560
That's a given.

135
00:05:37,560 --> 00:05:39,560
So why don't you go and rack up lots of debt

136
00:05:39,560 --> 00:05:42,040
to eat at nice restaurants when you're in medical school?

137
00:05:42,040 --> 00:05:44,560
And have a nice car and live in a nice apartment.

138
00:05:44,560 --> 00:05:47,640
Why not go and rack up debt for all of these things,

139
00:05:47,640 --> 00:05:50,680
and then pay it off out of your $300,000 or $400,000 a year

140
00:05:50,680 --> 00:05:53,160
salary once you're done?

141
00:05:53,160 --> 00:05:54,360
Why not?

142
00:05:54,360 --> 00:05:57,080
Why not?

143
00:05:57,080 --> 00:05:59,520
But when people actually look at what medical students do,

144
00:05:59,520 --> 00:06:01,720
they see that there is a very predictable rise

145
00:06:01,720 --> 00:06:05,480
in consumption right when they start working.

146
00:06:05,480 --> 00:06:06,960
So you start working, and then it's

147
00:06:06,960 --> 00:06:11,280
like, oh, fine, time to go and buy a nice car, buy nice stuff,

148
00:06:11,280 --> 00:06:14,440
get a nice apartment, now that I finished medical school.

149
00:06:14,440 --> 00:06:16,240
In fact, this is such a standard reaction

150
00:06:16,240 --> 00:06:19,200
that we don't really think about it as weird.

151
00:06:19,200 --> 00:06:20,880
But it is weird.

152
00:06:20,880 --> 00:06:22,440
One thing, if finishing medical school

153
00:06:22,440 --> 00:06:24,640
was like a one in 100 event, then

154
00:06:24,640 --> 00:06:26,840
finishing medical school is like winning the lottery.

155
00:06:26,840 --> 00:06:29,960
Then it does make sense to go and ramp up your lifestyle

156
00:06:29,960 --> 00:06:32,320
a lot if you happen to finish.

157
00:06:32,320 --> 00:06:35,440
But if you're almost certain to finish, then it's weird.

158
00:06:35,440 --> 00:06:38,800
So it was almost certain that it was going to happen.

159
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:42,400
So why weren't you already living like a doctor?

160
00:06:42,400 --> 00:06:44,000
Why not live like a doctor early?

161
00:06:47,560 --> 00:06:50,480
And so I'm as guilty of this as anyone.

162
00:06:50,480 --> 00:06:54,560
So when I was in grad school, I lived so poorly

163
00:06:54,560 --> 00:06:56,000
in terms of materially.

164
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:58,720
So there was one where actually it was so dumb,

165
00:06:58,720 --> 00:07:02,040
you can't even explain it by current income, by deviation.

166
00:07:02,040 --> 00:07:05,480
So the day after I got married, I changed my own oil on a day

167
00:07:05,480 --> 00:07:07,520
we were supposed to be packing for the honeymoon.

168
00:07:07,520 --> 00:07:09,480
And so we didn't wind up leaving until midnight

169
00:07:09,480 --> 00:07:12,720
because I am not actually good at changing oil.

170
00:07:12,720 --> 00:07:14,440
And it was a really hard day.

171
00:07:14,440 --> 00:07:18,320
And this is one where we had a giant bag of cash gifts.

172
00:07:18,320 --> 00:07:20,440
And I could have simply gone in there

173
00:07:20,440 --> 00:07:24,680
and taken 1% of that money out and taken it to Jiffy Lube

174
00:07:24,680 --> 00:07:26,200
and had it done.

175
00:07:26,200 --> 00:07:28,800
The only excuse I can plead is that I

176
00:07:28,800 --> 00:07:31,520
was raised in a family where no one ever changed.

177
00:07:31,520 --> 00:07:33,280
My dad always changed our oil.

178
00:07:33,280 --> 00:07:35,440
And so I was just not really familiar with the idea

179
00:07:35,440 --> 00:07:37,000
of paying people for this stuff.

180
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:38,240
Oh, you didn't.

181
00:07:38,240 --> 00:07:40,120
If Sarbhati showed up and I said,

182
00:07:40,120 --> 00:07:43,920
you are aware there are places that do this, right?

183
00:07:43,920 --> 00:07:46,200
Then I wouldn't have said no.

184
00:07:46,200 --> 00:07:47,000
I didn't know that.

185
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:48,000
I would have said yes.

186
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:50,280
And you realize that you are miserable now.

187
00:07:50,280 --> 00:07:52,120
Yes, I know.

188
00:07:52,120 --> 00:07:55,600
You realize that there is a big basket of money in there.

189
00:07:55,600 --> 00:07:56,560
Yes.

190
00:07:56,560 --> 00:07:58,840
So do you see where I'm going with this?

191
00:07:58,840 --> 00:08:01,000
And I think I would have done it if someone had just

192
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:02,760
come and helped me in this way.

193
00:08:02,760 --> 00:08:04,840
And I kept changing my own oil in grad school

194
00:08:04,840 --> 00:08:06,280
for a while, actually.

195
00:08:06,280 --> 00:08:08,200
I blame my friends for not making fun of me

196
00:08:08,200 --> 00:08:10,160
for my economic illiteracy.

197
00:08:10,160 --> 00:08:12,040
If one of them had walked by and just said,

198
00:08:12,040 --> 00:08:17,320
ha ha, you're working for less than minimum wage,

199
00:08:17,320 --> 00:08:18,960
that would have been a big help to me.

200
00:08:18,960 --> 00:08:21,120
But no one did it.

201
00:08:21,120 --> 00:08:24,520
And so I worked for less than minimum wage,

202
00:08:24,520 --> 00:08:29,720
changing oil, which is almost the worst job I've ever had.

203
00:08:29,720 --> 00:08:34,920
Anyway, so this is something where I am a sinner right now.

204
00:08:34,920 --> 00:08:36,800
Repented, but.

205
00:08:36,800 --> 00:08:39,160
So I'm helping you now.

206
00:08:39,160 --> 00:08:40,760
So just imagine me laughing at you

207
00:08:40,760 --> 00:08:42,960
if you're working for less than minimum wage,

208
00:08:42,960 --> 00:08:44,600
doing something that you don't enjoy.

209
00:08:47,880 --> 00:08:49,480
Anyway, consumption does seem moderately

210
00:08:49,480 --> 00:08:50,960
sensitive to current income.

211
00:08:50,960 --> 00:08:52,560
There's an old paper by Greg Mankiw

212
00:08:52,560 --> 00:08:56,160
where he estimates a model where half the people just

213
00:08:56,160 --> 00:08:58,920
consume whatever they consume out of current income

214
00:08:58,920 --> 00:09:00,560
and half do permanent income.

215
00:09:00,560 --> 00:09:01,520
So essentially, it's one where there's

216
00:09:01,520 --> 00:09:03,520
just two types of consumers, in other words.

217
00:09:03,520 --> 00:09:05,600
Realistically, there'd be a continuum of people.

218
00:09:05,600 --> 00:09:07,560
But anyway, in a model where you assume

219
00:09:07,560 --> 00:09:10,760
that either you are a dummy who just mechanically consumes

220
00:09:10,760 --> 00:09:13,400
out of current income or you're Milton Friedman,

221
00:09:13,400 --> 00:09:15,960
then his estimate is half the population is Milton Friedman

222
00:09:15,960 --> 00:09:17,800
and half is dumb.

223
00:09:17,800 --> 00:09:22,000
So it gives you some idea.

224
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:24,280
Further result is that consumption responses seem

225
00:09:24,280 --> 00:09:27,040
to vary with the nature of the income.

226
00:09:27,040 --> 00:09:29,680
Consumption responses vary the nature of the income.

227
00:09:29,680 --> 00:09:32,920
So when people get a one-time cash bonus,

228
00:09:32,920 --> 00:09:37,320
then they are likely to use it to buy a one-time big cash

229
00:09:37,320 --> 00:09:39,200
thing.

230
00:09:39,200 --> 00:09:40,360
So you get a bonus.

231
00:09:40,360 --> 00:09:42,080
A lot of people just take the whole bonus

232
00:09:42,080 --> 00:09:45,440
and buy something with it, which again, is not

233
00:09:45,440 --> 00:09:48,440
what the permanent income hypothesis would predict.

234
00:09:48,440 --> 00:09:50,720
Normal prediction would be that if you get a cash

235
00:09:50,720 --> 00:09:53,560
bonus of $1,000 you didn't expect,

236
00:09:53,560 --> 00:09:56,440
this will have the same effect as if your expected lifetime

237
00:09:56,440 --> 00:10:00,720
income goes up by that amount of present value.

238
00:10:00,720 --> 00:10:02,080
Yes?

239
00:10:02,080 --> 00:10:05,040
Why can't you explain something like the medical school

240
00:10:05,040 --> 00:10:08,240
thing with sort of like an expected value kind of thing?

241
00:10:08,240 --> 00:10:11,600
Like the probability that you don't finish medical school

242
00:10:11,600 --> 00:10:13,080
might be really small.

243
00:10:13,080 --> 00:10:16,160
But if it happens and you spent, you took out a whole bunch

244
00:10:16,160 --> 00:10:19,000
of loans to live lavishly while you're in,

245
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:21,280
like you're really screwed and your life's going to be

246
00:10:21,280 --> 00:10:26,040
terrible, but how bad it is to just sort of not

247
00:10:26,040 --> 00:10:27,740
eat at a nice restaurant for a few years

248
00:10:27,740 --> 00:10:31,160
while you're in medical school isn't nearly probably as bad

249
00:10:31,160 --> 00:10:34,280
as the rest of your life would be if you have all that debt

250
00:10:34,280 --> 00:10:37,360
and for whatever reason you're in the 5% that doesn't finish.

251
00:10:37,360 --> 00:10:37,760
Yeah.

252
00:10:37,760 --> 00:10:40,160
So the answer there is just like with the expected utility

253
00:10:40,160 --> 00:10:41,880
theory in general, the problem is

254
00:10:41,880 --> 00:10:45,040
there seems to be this excess sensitivity to a few percent

255
00:10:45,040 --> 00:10:46,280
risk.

256
00:10:46,280 --> 00:10:48,560
So it seems like, again, not much difference

257
00:10:48,560 --> 00:10:50,960
in the response between someone who has a 1% chance of not

258
00:10:50,960 --> 00:10:53,160
finishing and a 5% or a 10% or a 20%.

259
00:10:53,160 --> 00:10:56,040
In both cases, you finish and then cha-ching,

260
00:10:56,040 --> 00:10:59,640
whereas if it were just a simple expected utility model,

261
00:10:59,640 --> 00:11:00,960
then you're right.

262
00:11:00,960 --> 00:11:03,320
There would be some response, but it

263
00:11:03,320 --> 00:11:05,760
should depend upon how much uncertainty is being resolved.

264
00:11:05,760 --> 00:11:08,720
And that seems like there's a big range where

265
00:11:08,720 --> 00:11:09,760
there's any difference.

266
00:11:09,760 --> 00:11:11,560
And again, remember that just the fact

267
00:11:11,560 --> 00:11:15,680
that you're a doctor does not actually guarantee you anything.

268
00:11:15,680 --> 00:11:18,120
And yet people sort of act like, well, now I'm a doctor.

269
00:11:18,120 --> 00:11:19,200
Now everything is sure.

270
00:11:19,200 --> 00:11:20,880
It's like, well, what if you lose your license

271
00:11:20,880 --> 00:11:22,160
because you made a mistake?

272
00:11:22,160 --> 00:11:23,660
And then you build up a lot of debt,

273
00:11:23,660 --> 00:11:25,400
and then you're in big trouble.

274
00:11:25,400 --> 00:11:28,200
But that one doesn't seem to bother people the same way.

275
00:11:28,200 --> 00:11:28,840
Yes.

276
00:11:28,840 --> 00:11:30,800
So again, there's these pseudo-certainty effects

277
00:11:30,800 --> 00:11:33,960
where you reach a social milestone of, I finished.

278
00:11:33,960 --> 00:11:35,080
Now I am a doctor.

279
00:11:35,080 --> 00:11:37,000
Now I'm entitled to spend.

280
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,720
It's like, well, yeah, but so many things could go wrong.

281
00:11:39,720 --> 00:11:42,560
You could get in a terrible accident.

282
00:11:42,560 --> 00:11:45,480
Like if you're a surgeon, your hands could get paralyzed

283
00:11:45,480 --> 00:11:46,960
and just list off all the things.

284
00:11:46,960 --> 00:11:52,120
So even then, there'd still be reasons for you to go and take

285
00:11:52,120 --> 00:11:53,160
extra precautions.

286
00:11:53,160 --> 00:11:54,920
And yet there's a lot of these where

287
00:11:54,920 --> 00:12:00,080
people seem to not be planning much at all, if at all.

288
00:12:00,080 --> 00:12:01,240
All right.

289
00:12:01,240 --> 00:12:02,080
Now, yes.

290
00:12:02,080 --> 00:12:04,560
You used to say that people are loss averse.

291
00:12:04,560 --> 00:12:07,880
So in the back, they usually take a larger risk

292
00:12:07,880 --> 00:12:11,040
than they exactly have.

293
00:12:11,040 --> 00:12:13,720
So actually, what I said is that many behavioral economists

294
00:12:13,720 --> 00:12:15,720
think that people are loss averse.

295
00:12:15,720 --> 00:12:18,960
Yeah, so again, that would mean that, yes.

296
00:12:18,960 --> 00:12:21,080
So actually, that seems strange.

297
00:12:21,080 --> 00:12:22,960
Because remember, what loss aversion

298
00:12:22,960 --> 00:12:25,960
says is that you'll be risk preferring for losses.

299
00:12:25,960 --> 00:12:27,560
So actually, this should mean that you

300
00:12:27,560 --> 00:12:30,840
shouldn't be worrying about a small chance

301
00:12:30,840 --> 00:12:33,240
that you don't finish medical school.

302
00:12:33,240 --> 00:12:34,720
At least that's the simple version.

303
00:12:34,720 --> 00:12:36,920
And remember, there's the fourfold version,

304
00:12:36,920 --> 00:12:40,160
which makes it more complicated.

305
00:12:40,160 --> 00:12:41,400
All right.

306
00:12:41,400 --> 00:12:42,560
Yes.

307
00:12:42,560 --> 00:12:45,760
So it is possible that a better life

308
00:12:45,760 --> 00:12:49,320
is that kind of life purpose.

309
00:12:49,320 --> 00:12:54,840
If I already live very well now, I lose my purpose of living.

310
00:12:54,840 --> 00:12:56,760
So I would like to leave some space

311
00:12:56,760 --> 00:13:00,360
that will work harder to reach a better life in the future.

312
00:13:00,360 --> 00:13:00,920
Right, right.

313
00:13:00,920 --> 00:13:03,400
So that means that you're sort of getting,

314
00:13:03,400 --> 00:13:05,360
at least in a general sense, you're

315
00:13:05,360 --> 00:13:06,880
getting rid of a simple diminishing

316
00:13:06,880 --> 00:13:08,880
marginal utility consumption model.

317
00:13:08,880 --> 00:13:11,280
And instead, it's one where at any point in time

318
00:13:11,280 --> 00:13:12,400
it's diminishing.

319
00:13:12,400 --> 00:13:14,360
But over my life, I want it to be rising.

320
00:13:14,360 --> 00:13:16,920
So I feel like my life is improving.

321
00:13:16,920 --> 00:13:20,080
So that again would give you a very different model

322
00:13:20,080 --> 00:13:20,880
than the usual one.

323
00:13:20,880 --> 00:13:24,440
But that makes quite a bit of sense

324
00:13:24,440 --> 00:13:27,840
as a description of people's behavior.

325
00:13:27,840 --> 00:13:30,080
All right, now if you have durable goods,

326
00:13:30,080 --> 00:13:32,720
this gives you a little bit of extra room.

327
00:13:32,720 --> 00:13:34,400
But even there, not too much.

328
00:13:34,400 --> 00:13:35,860
Again, so when you add durable goods

329
00:13:35,860 --> 00:13:37,760
into the permanent income hypothesis,

330
00:13:37,760 --> 00:13:41,800
this says that it's basically an optimal stock of durable goods

331
00:13:41,800 --> 00:13:45,000
that you want to have given whatever your expected lifetime

332
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:46,320
income is.

333
00:13:46,320 --> 00:13:49,160
And this means that if suddenly there's some new news,

334
00:13:49,160 --> 00:13:51,680
you may want to go and buy nicer durable goods.

335
00:13:51,680 --> 00:13:53,140
But again, the problem is it seems

336
00:13:53,140 --> 00:13:56,000
like people also have this excess sensitivity

337
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:57,400
for current consumption.

338
00:13:57,400 --> 00:14:00,320
It's like going to restaurants and things like that.

339
00:14:00,320 --> 00:14:02,200
So it is true that if you find out

340
00:14:02,200 --> 00:14:04,560
that you're going to inherit some money, that is a reason

341
00:14:04,560 --> 00:14:06,840
to go and buy a car.

342
00:14:06,840 --> 00:14:08,840
Or even if you find out that you're

343
00:14:08,840 --> 00:14:11,040
going to get a 10% raise, that is a reason

344
00:14:11,040 --> 00:14:13,120
to go out and buy a new car today because you see,

345
00:14:13,120 --> 00:14:16,120
well, now it makes sense for my stock of durable goods

346
00:14:16,120 --> 00:14:17,920
to go up.

347
00:14:17,920 --> 00:14:20,120
But anyway, so durable goods do make some difference.

348
00:14:20,120 --> 00:14:22,080
And with durable goods, this is the main way

349
00:14:22,080 --> 00:14:23,680
where in a simple model it can still

350
00:14:23,680 --> 00:14:26,600
make sense for you to run out and buy a really nice thing

351
00:14:26,600 --> 00:14:27,820
when you get a raise.

352
00:14:27,820 --> 00:14:29,280
But it's got to be a durable thing.

353
00:14:29,280 --> 00:14:30,600
So in the model, it doesn't make sense

354
00:14:30,600 --> 00:14:32,520
to go to a nice restaurant when you get a raise.

355
00:14:32,520 --> 00:14:35,240
It doesn't make sense to get a car or get a better car

356
00:14:35,240 --> 00:14:38,360
because the restaurant is the current consumption,

357
00:14:38,360 --> 00:14:42,320
whereas the car is a durable good.

358
00:14:42,320 --> 00:14:44,720
And furthermore, also empirical evidence

359
00:14:44,720 --> 00:14:48,120
that people are reluctant to borrow against or tap

360
00:14:48,120 --> 00:14:52,840
into pensions and home equity to smooth their current income.

361
00:14:52,840 --> 00:14:55,360
So if you have home equity, if you have any home equity

362
00:14:55,360 --> 00:15:02,680
built up, you actually have a fantastic source of credit,

363
00:15:02,680 --> 00:15:04,960
which is you just borrow against your home equity.

364
00:15:04,960 --> 00:15:07,120
And the rate is ridiculously low,

365
00:15:07,120 --> 00:15:09,480
especially adjusting for taxes.

366
00:15:09,480 --> 00:15:13,720
So very common rate if you have a decent amount of home equity

367
00:15:13,720 --> 00:15:16,080
would be like prime rate minus a half.

368
00:15:16,080 --> 00:15:19,880
And then all that interest is deductible on your taxes.

369
00:15:19,880 --> 00:15:22,800
The Trump tax reform changed a little bit, but not that much.

370
00:15:22,800 --> 00:15:25,880
And so actually, there's a lot of people

371
00:15:25,880 --> 00:15:27,560
who effectively have a negative real rate

372
00:15:27,560 --> 00:15:30,320
they can borrow against up to an enormous amount of money,

373
00:15:30,320 --> 00:15:31,480
especially if they've been older and they've

374
00:15:31,480 --> 00:15:32,920
been building up equity.

375
00:15:32,920 --> 00:15:34,840
Now, there was a question back there.

376
00:15:34,840 --> 00:15:38,280
Yeah, is that historically true?

377
00:15:38,280 --> 00:15:41,160
Like pre-2007, say?

378
00:15:41,160 --> 00:15:45,080
That you could do this, that it was possible to go and borrow

379
00:15:45,080 --> 00:15:46,640
against home equity?

380
00:15:46,640 --> 00:15:48,120
No, no, no.

381
00:15:48,120 --> 00:15:51,000
Oh, that people are reluctant to do it?

382
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:51,520
Yeah.

383
00:15:51,520 --> 00:15:52,020
Yes.

384
00:15:52,020 --> 00:15:54,160
So historically, people were definitely reluctant.

385
00:15:54,160 --> 00:15:59,000
During the years up to the Great Recession,

386
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:01,320
it seemed like some of the psychological barriers

387
00:16:01,320 --> 00:16:03,040
were starting to fall a bit.

388
00:16:03,040 --> 00:16:04,960
And then as to how much they've been restored,

389
00:16:04,960 --> 00:16:07,640
I'm not clear on this.

390
00:16:07,640 --> 00:16:09,720
So anyway, there is this reluctance

391
00:16:09,720 --> 00:16:13,160
to tap into things that are called savings,

392
00:16:13,160 --> 00:16:17,480
even when it's a really low interest rate.

393
00:16:17,480 --> 00:16:19,800
And you could say, well, yeah, it's called savings.

394
00:16:19,800 --> 00:16:22,760
But what's the difference between something

395
00:16:22,760 --> 00:16:25,280
called savings and something not called savings if they're

396
00:16:25,280 --> 00:16:26,360
both the same?

397
00:16:26,360 --> 00:16:30,600
And again, there are people who are so silly as to have,

398
00:16:30,600 --> 00:16:35,280
say, $30,000 in credit card debt and $100,000 in home equity.

399
00:16:35,280 --> 00:16:37,560
And there's a very simple financial transaction

400
00:16:37,560 --> 00:16:40,760
you can make to greatly reduce the amount of interest

401
00:16:40,760 --> 00:16:43,120
you have to pay while maintaining everything else.

402
00:16:43,120 --> 00:16:45,240
So if you've got $100,000 in home equity, $30,000

403
00:16:45,240 --> 00:16:47,960
in credit card debt, what is the simple operation

404
00:16:47,960 --> 00:16:51,360
that you would be well advised to try?

405
00:16:51,360 --> 00:16:53,360
Use the home equity to pay off your credit card.

406
00:16:53,360 --> 00:16:53,360
Yes.

407
00:16:53,360 --> 00:16:55,060
Borrow $30,000 to get your home equity,

408
00:16:55,060 --> 00:16:57,880
pay off your credit card.

409
00:16:57,880 --> 00:17:00,840
Because the credit card debt interest rate is,

410
00:17:00,840 --> 00:17:07,520
in real terms, actually, maybe 1,000 times the rates.

411
00:17:07,520 --> 00:17:09,080
That you are paying on home equity.

412
00:17:09,080 --> 00:17:10,800
Home equity really could be a negative.

413
00:17:10,800 --> 00:17:12,960
Actually, it could easily be a negative rate.

414
00:17:12,960 --> 00:17:16,240
And the credit card, probably a 15% rate.

415
00:17:16,240 --> 00:17:18,120
So and yet, well, why not?

416
00:17:18,120 --> 00:17:20,440
Well, but that's my home equity.

417
00:17:20,440 --> 00:17:23,560
That's like my special status of protection.

418
00:17:23,560 --> 00:17:25,120
And this credit card, well, that's

419
00:17:25,120 --> 00:17:28,160
the monument to my profligacy.

420
00:17:28,160 --> 00:17:33,440
But let's keep it there to remind me of my sin.

421
00:17:33,440 --> 00:17:35,280
And again, so you guys are familiar with what

422
00:17:35,280 --> 00:17:37,240
a reverse mortgage is?

423
00:17:37,240 --> 00:17:38,800
Yeah, so there's some comedians who

424
00:17:38,800 --> 00:17:40,400
have talked about it in general.

425
00:17:40,400 --> 00:17:41,920
But they make it sound stupid.

426
00:17:41,920 --> 00:17:43,680
But it's actually a really good idea.

427
00:17:43,680 --> 00:17:45,600
And with a normal mortgage, there's

428
00:17:45,600 --> 00:17:50,120
a person who wants a house, but they don't have money.

429
00:17:50,120 --> 00:17:52,820
So they go and they say, look, if you give me a house,

430
00:17:52,820 --> 00:17:55,680
I will agree to go and pay the bank money every month

431
00:17:55,680 --> 00:17:58,000
for 20 or 30 years.

432
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:01,600
And with a reverse mortgage, you start out owning a house.

433
00:18:01,600 --> 00:18:03,640
And you want the bank to give you money.

434
00:18:03,640 --> 00:18:05,200
So you say, tell you what, you give me

435
00:18:05,200 --> 00:18:06,360
money for 20 or 30 years.

436
00:18:06,360 --> 00:18:07,960
And then at the end, you get a house.

437
00:18:07,960 --> 00:18:09,540
Usually, they're structured so that you

438
00:18:09,540 --> 00:18:11,120
get money until you die.

439
00:18:11,120 --> 00:18:13,560
And then the bank gets the house.

440
00:18:13,560 --> 00:18:16,080
And then normally, of course, they have extra revisions.

441
00:18:16,080 --> 00:18:17,800
Like if you die in a month, then the bank

442
00:18:17,800 --> 00:18:19,000
doesn't get the entire house.

443
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:21,040
Although there's a good reason to actually sign

444
00:18:21,040 --> 00:18:23,160
a contract saying the bank can get the entire house

445
00:18:23,160 --> 00:18:26,080
if you die next month, which is they'll give you more money.

446
00:18:26,080 --> 00:18:28,160
They'll give you more money if they get this more

447
00:18:28,160 --> 00:18:29,240
favorable contract.

448
00:18:29,240 --> 00:18:32,720
Anyway, so reverse mortgage has originally been very unpopular.

449
00:18:32,720 --> 00:18:35,800
And even for the people who make a lot of sense for them

450
00:18:35,800 --> 00:18:38,840
to do those, to get them, it's like elderly people

451
00:18:38,840 --> 00:18:42,400
who are living very modestly, sometimes eating dog food,

452
00:18:42,400 --> 00:18:45,640
in homes of great value.

453
00:18:45,640 --> 00:18:47,640
So put that together, and the reverse mortgage

454
00:18:47,640 --> 00:18:49,720
makes a great deal of sense.

455
00:18:49,720 --> 00:18:51,100
This is another case, by the way,

456
00:18:51,100 --> 00:18:53,840
where the regulatory response to people underusing

457
00:18:53,840 --> 00:18:56,920
reverse mortgages has been to try to prevent

458
00:18:56,920 --> 00:18:59,240
more reverse mortgages.

459
00:18:59,240 --> 00:19:00,880
So government regulators generally

460
00:19:00,880 --> 00:19:02,600
have the view that only a dummy would ever

461
00:19:02,600 --> 00:19:05,160
want a reverse mortgage because you're losing a house.

462
00:19:05,160 --> 00:19:07,360
And so you're just taking advantage of the elderly.

463
00:19:07,360 --> 00:19:09,160
Rather, the more sensible view of it's just

464
00:19:09,160 --> 00:19:11,560
stubborn elderly people who don't realize reverse mortgage

465
00:19:11,560 --> 00:19:13,720
isn't right for them, which I would say

466
00:19:13,720 --> 00:19:16,000
would be a more reasonable thing would be for regulators

467
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:18,560
to be trying to ram the reverse mortgages down people's

468
00:19:18,560 --> 00:19:21,120
throats and sending them things saying,

469
00:19:21,120 --> 00:19:24,040
unless you fill out these, if you write an essay explaining

470
00:19:24,040 --> 00:19:26,920
why you don't want one, you get one.

471
00:19:26,920 --> 00:19:28,400
And then it gets graded by someone

472
00:19:28,400 --> 00:19:29,600
on your financial literacy.

473
00:19:29,600 --> 00:19:31,520
So just make sure that you understand

474
00:19:31,520 --> 00:19:34,480
what a great opportunity you're missing out on.

475
00:19:34,480 --> 00:19:35,600
Probably some of it is people's kids

476
00:19:35,600 --> 00:19:37,480
are mad that their parents get to live nicely

477
00:19:37,480 --> 00:19:39,720
and they don't get to inherit a house.

478
00:19:39,720 --> 00:19:42,720
Those are called bad kids.

479
00:19:42,720 --> 00:19:44,920
Regretting this to your parents.

480
00:19:44,920 --> 00:19:51,880
So this does bring me to my very favorite conversation

481
00:19:51,880 --> 00:19:54,800
about my dad's economic illiteracy, reflective of it.

482
00:19:54,800 --> 00:19:59,600
So this is like around 2005, 2006.

483
00:19:59,600 --> 00:20:05,600
The homeless.


